  "catalogue": "5 DEV source sequences on 5 distinct cameras, cut into 10 windows (5 fire, 5 non-fire) of 31 frames each, 310 frames in total.",
  "selection": [
    "The DEV windows sorted by sequence_id, first per camera, first 5. A rule and not a hand-picked list, so the micro corpus is reproducible from the committed DEV manifest alone.",
    "DEV and never HOLD: the micro corpus is the one part of the imagery that is committed and therefore looked at, and HOLD is read exactly once.",
    "Each source sequence is then cut in two at its ignition frame. A FIgLib window spans -2400 s to +2400 s around ignition, so its pre-ignition half is a real non-fire camera-night on the same camera at the same time of day. The fire half keeps 8 pre-ignition frames of lead-in so the background model is warm at t0; the non-fire half gives those frames up, so the two halves share no frame."
  ],
  "calibration_note": "THE ONE THING TO READ BEFORE QUOTING ANY NUMBER FROM THIS ARM: micro's calibration windows are the pre-ignition halves of its own fire sequences. They are real non-fire pixels on the real cameras, but they are not an independent adversary -- the CONFUSE arm is, and it is 33 windows chosen by confusion class in data/manifest_confuse.json. So the micro arm proves that the pipeline runs and that its committed numbers are self-consistent, and it is NOT and never claims to be the held-out headline: that is the 54 held-out sequences that survive exclusion -- data/manifest_hold.json names 60 windows, data/excluded.json drops 6 of them. Producing data/excluded.json meant a human looking at the FIRST FRAME of every HOLD window, about 40 minutes before ignition, against criteria stated before the pass; the HOLD access log in results/ still records no HOLD read, because no HOLD pixel has been scored., measured by `make fetch && make bench`. Nor is its time-to-detection an estimate of anything: five fire windows is too few for a median, and the count that detects at all is chaotic with respect to noise far below one grey level -- re-encoding these same frames at JPEG quality 92/95/98/100 gives 0, 2, 2 and 1 detections of 5 and moves the solved threshold from 0.30 to 0.41, with identical code and an identical cut. What reproduces here is the bytes, not the science.",
  "sha256_note": "These frames are committed, so git is their receipt. data/fetch_receipt.json covers the fetched arms only.",
